 as to should this or  shouldn't this count  as a coronavirus death but these are  small numbers of situations  in a country with 5.2 million infections  you're bound to have some anecdotes like  that but  on the other side there's a much more  compelling reason to think the real  coronavirus death toll  is dramatically higher than the official  and it is something called  excess deaths the number of extra deaths  during the march to july period suggests  that more than 54 000 additional people  have died of coronavirus be above and  beyond the  uh official death toll this is derived  when you look at  excess deaths and in the united states  because we have so many people and so  many years of data  and such detailed statistics about  disease  we know how many people on average will  die  of all causes based on our population  based on what's called the normal amount  of death  we know okay if you add up expected car  accident deaths cancer deaths heart  disease flu altogether  we know when you don't have a pandemic  how many people typically die  and if you look at the deaths we would  expect from march 15th  to july 25th you see that during those  dates there were almost 201 000  excess deaths deaths beyond what we  would normally have  and during that time the official  coronavirus death toll  was only 147 000. that leaves about  54 000 extra deaths not officially  attributed  to coronavirus now epidemiologists  favor looking at excess deaths  to gauge the impact of a pandemic  because excess deaths don't depend on  testing people and knowing exactly who  had the virus  posthumous testing testing when someone  goes into the hospital  etc and indeed when you look at excess  deaths everything tracks consistently  with this theory if you look at where  the excess deaths disproportionately are  it's in the state's hardest hit by  coronavirus and it is a very coherent  story that excess death analysis points  us to so in all likelihood  the real coronavirus death toll